## Proposed triage rule and next actions

The [accepted 2024 decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) recommend merging by subject. Their text is contradicted by the executable
examples: identical subjects can have different causes, and different subjects can share one cause.
Their review dates are old; `accepted` is not evidence that the recommendation still matches the system.

Keep that accepted decision intact. For the desk's review, propose a new decision record that groups by verified
cause after checking tenant locale, form version and channel. An unknown tenant remains unplaced until investigated.
Matching a cause groups reports for an engineering issue; it does not prove two different report IDs are the same
submission. Deduplicate the export by report ID first.

Open the parser and hint tickets separately, keep the one-day-move notes visible, and verify any new live reports
against the tenant directory before merging them. Rebuild this page when the data changes: its counts are computed,
while the issue bodies are dated snapshots and must be regenerated and reviewed.

<Callout kind="warn">
The support desk can keep receiving reports after this export. Do not treat a known status, an intake tag,
or a historical merge as proof that every report has the same cause or that a queued fix is complete.
</Callout>

## Evidence and reproducibility

Run `npm ci --ignore-scripts`, then `npm run audit` and `npm run build` from this exercise folder.
The audit checks duplicate content, report coverage and an independent classification against the supplied rule.
The build runs the actual mdsvex and Svelte components and saves the page the desk will see, including this signature.
Original source files are preserved; the derived ledger, issue drafts and render are identified in `DELIVERY.md`.

